[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two simple, round, matte-white ceramic bowls, one in the immediate foreground and the second slightly blurred in the background, both centered in the frame with generous negative space. The camera is positioned at a 3/4 overhead angle, perfectly capturing the vibrant, colorful contents of the salad. The depth of field is shallow, leaving the foreground bowl tack-sharp. Soft natural light streams from a window on the left, casting subtle, gentle shadows and highlighting the fresh sheen of the extra-virgin olive oil coating the ingredients. The setting is a worn, rustic aged oak kitchen table with visible wood grain. Props include a wooden spoon resting casually beside the primary bowl, a crumpled linen napkin tucked partially beneath the bowl, and a few stray pieces of cherry tomato and a sprig of fresh parsley scattered naturally on the table surface. The salad is a bright, textured mixture of beige, cooked black-eyed peas, halved cherry tomatoes with their glossy, juicy skins, crisp emerald green diced cucumber, and flecks of finely chopped purple red onion and fresh, vivid green parsley. The dressing provides a faint, shimmering with olive oil and tiny specks of dried oregano and black pepper, coats every ingredient. The appearance is fresh, rustic, and inviting, with slight natural imperfections like an uneven distribution of ingredients and a few stray herbs clinging to the bowl's rim. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
